The testing harness (../../runall) doesn't run these files directly, but
instead through .mlb files in the parent directory. This is because the test
command is simply `smlfmt --preview-only`, which does not enable SML# syntax.
But we can use a .mlb file to turn it on.

See the file ../smlsharp.mlb:

  $(SML_LIB)/basis/basis.mlb
  ann "allowSMLSharp true" in
    smlsharp/basic.sml
  end

This allows us to test SML# syntax while using the same test command for all
test files.

Every file here should be accepted by the real SML# compiler. Not everything
the grammar derives is accepted: for example a flexible record type is
rejected in a `type` declaration, so it only appears as a type annotation.
Check a file with:

  echo '_require "basis.smi"' > foo.smi
  smlsharp -c foo.sml

Two of the files need a different check. `_use` is only accepted in an
interactive session, so use.sml is checked with `smlsharp < use.sml`.
`_interface` is only accepted when compiling, and interface.sml needs a
matching interface.smi next to it.

Note that SML# interface files (.smi) are a different grammar and are not
covered here.
